<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Inferno (Dante)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Inferno is the first part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante Alighieri</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/divine-comedy">Divine Comedy</a>, written in the early 14th century. It describes Dante's journey, guided by the Roman poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Virgil</a>, through the nine circles of Hell, where he meets sinners from history and from the Italian politics of his day. Among its best-known episodes is the story of the lovers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesca-da-rimini">Francesca da Rimini</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paolo-malatesta">Paolo Malatesta</a> in the fifth canto.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/inferno-dante.jpg" alt="Inferno (Dante)" width="320" height="457" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Inferno.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
